# Round XXVII Track D: `Set[T].enumerate()` in a `for` loop is a check-time
# REJECT. Per docs (`docs/book/05-collections.md:198-207` +
# `lib/std/iter.gg:307/814`), `.enumerate()` is an `Iterator[T]` adapter and
# Set impls `Iterable[T]` only (it has `.iter()` but is not itself an
# iterator). Pre-D27 the `for i, x in s.enumerate():` scaffold reached
# `lower_for_enumerate`, read `iter.Field(2)` (Vector/Str's `len` slot)
# against the hash-table layout, and terminated the counter loop before
# emitting any entry — silent zero output on BOTH C+LLVM (Core #10
# lower-or-reject class). D27 rejects at check-time with a pointer to
# `.iter().enumerate()`.
#
# Set REQUIRES `@derive(Equatable, Hashable)` on the struct payload.
@derive(Equatable, Hashable)
struct P:
    int x
    int y

int main():
    Set[P] s = {}
    s.add(P(1, 2))
    s.add(P(3, 4))
    # Rejected at check-time: `.enumerate()` requires Iterator[T]; Set is
    # Iterable[T] only. Fix per the error's pointer: `s.iter().enumerate()`.
    for i, p in s.enumerate():
        print(f"{i}:{p.x}/{p.y}")
    return 0
